# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in. Eve auto-loads .env and .env.local.
# Full deployment runbook (access config + safety rules): docs/deploy/setup-runbook.md.
#
# ⚠️ MANY OF THE COMMENTED-OUT KNOBS BELOW ARE NO LONGER THE PRIMARY WAY TO SET THEM (OPE-371).
# Agent BEHAVIOUR — which walls are up, which apparatus is armed, what the caps are — is typed
# code: the schema is packages/core/agent/settings/settings.ts and this deployment's overrides go
# in agent/settings.ts, so a behaviour change is a reviewable diff instead of an invisible
# dashboard toggle. Each such variable survives as a declared OPS OVERRIDE (it still wins, still
# resolved fresh on each read, so a kill switch works without a redeploy) — but prefer the diff.
# `docs/deploy/configuration-reference.md § Agent settings` is the generated table of every one,
# including the handful that are read once at boot rather than per call.
# What stays env, and belongs here: connection targets, the bootstrap credentials that reach a
# store, and the OE_E2E_*/E2E_* harness seams. The deployment's own identity — its org, its ambient
# workspace, its ambient surface — is tracked config in `agent/identity/surfaces.ts`, not env
# (OPE-1545).
#
# ⚠️ SECRETS: the ENCRYPTED CREDENTIAL STORE IS THE DEFAULT HOME, not this file (OPE-408). Deploy
# with the root-of-trust vars only — DATABASE_URL + OE_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY (+
# OE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL) — then load every other secret with `oe secret set <NAME>` (value on stdin;
# `--from-file` for a whole .env's worth), or ask an agent to use the ops MCP server's `secret_set` (it takes names
# and reads each value from .env.local, where `oe env set <NAME>` put it).
# Both run on YOUR machine against YOUR database; nothing is readable back out afterwards.
# OE_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY is a base64-encoded 32-byte AES-256 key. Do not hand-roll one in a
# shell: the ops MCP server's `scaffold_credential_key` mints it straight into .env.local and never
# prints the value. See docs/deploy/agent-driven-setup.md § 7.
# Setting a secret below still WORKS and is the supported on-ramp — a credential naming
# `broker: "env"` reads it, and the channel-plumbing vars below are still read from the environment
# until their broker seams land. Treat those as removable, not as the home. See
# docs/deploy/setup-runbook.md § 3 Secrets and docs/security/credentials-and-planes.md.

# --- Model: Vercel AI Gateway ---
# Model ids are NOT set here: every model this deploy uses is named by ROLE in a model
# STACK (agent/model/stacks.ts), picked by `models.stack` in agent/settings/settings.ts.
# Auth is to the GATEWAY, not to the upstream provider. Create a key at:
#   Vercel dashboard -> AI Gateway -> API Keys
# (Alternatively run `eve link` to fetch Gateway creds automatically. On a deployed
#  Vercel project, OIDC is auto-injected and no key is needed.)
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# WHERE the gateway is lives in Git, not here: `models.targets.gateway.url` in
# agent/settings/settings.ts. The OE_AI_GATEWAY_URL variable was deleted (OPE-2650).

# Which upstream serves a call is a gateway-operator concern (AI Gateway -> Settings ->
# Restrict allowed providers), not application config — no per-provider key needed here.
#
# Model choice is NOT env (OPE-2411): the stack, the default model id and the hard pin are the
# `models.stack`, `models.primary` and `models.force` settings in agent/settings/settings.ts.
# (OE_MODEL_STACK, OE_PRIMARY_MODEL and OE_FORCE_MODEL are deleted; setting one does nothing.)

# --- GitHub personal access token — read ONLY when `github.auth` is "token" (OPE-2530) ---
# For an org that will not create a GitHub App. Set `github: { auth: "token" }` in
# agent/settings/settings.ts; the token then reaches only this deployment's own repo
# (`accessRequests.configRepo`) and `github.tokenRepos`. With the shipped `auth: "app"` it is never read,
# even when set — there is no fallback. Use a fine-grained token limited to those repos, with
# Contents and Pull requests read/write. See docs/capabilities/source-control.md.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# --- Source-control plane: GitHub App (agent/scm/) — OPTIONAL, the proper model ---
# Gives the agent its OWN identity (open-everywhere[bot]) so it clones granted repos and opens
# PRs authored by the app, using short-lived per-session repo-scoped installation tokens
# (minted host-side, never seen by the model). Self-host registers its OWN Open Everywhere App
# (the GHES model). Leave unset to run without source-control capability.
# OE_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# OE_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=          # preferred JWT issuer when present
# OE_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=        # PEM (PKCS#1 or #8) — server-side secret, never in a sandbox
# OE_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=open-everywhere        # → bot user "open-everywhere[bot]"
# OE_GITHUB_BOT_USER_ID=            # numeric id from GET /users/open-everywhere[bot] (cache once); for commit attribution
# owner → installation id. JSON map (multi-org) and/or the single-owner pair. OPTIONAL: GitHub
# delivers the `installation` webhook to every App by default (no subscription to switch on), so
# each org that installs registers itself; what you set here stays authoritative and is composed
# OVER the discovered entries.
# OE_GITHUB_INSTALLATIONS={"acme":"4242"}
# OE_GITHUB_OWNER=
# OE_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID=

# --- Access requests (docs/how-it-works/access-requests.md) — OPTIONAL, and not env ---
# The widen-slow path is configured in Git since OPE-2650: `accessRequests.adminChannel`,
# `accessRequests.configRepo` and `accessRequests.configBase` in agent/settings/settings.ts. The
# overlay paths, the e2e/test dry run and the live credential-binding PR are fields of
# agent/access/access-requests.ts. No variable in this file arms the flow.

# --- GitHub surface (agent/channels/github.ts) — INGRESS App: webhooks + reply-as-app ---
# GitHub is now a PROPER conversational surface: @mention the bot in an issue/PR/review
# comment and it wakes an agent session (repo checked out into the sandbox, PR diff in
# context) and replies as a comment. Same route ALSO runs the Phase-3 event→Slack
# routine fan-out (PR opened, CI failed, …). Point the GitHub App webhook at
# POST /eve/v1/github (content-type application/json), subscribing to issue_comment +
# pull_request_review_comment (for @mentions) and issues / pull_request / check_suite /
# check_run / workflow_run (for the routine fan-out).
#
# These are the eve-native ingress-App vars the channel falls back to. NOTE: this is the
# INGRESS app (receives webhooks, mints tokens to POST the reply comment + `eyes`
# reaction) and is ORTHOGONAL to the tool-side source-control/checkout credentials above
# (OE_GITHUB_APP_* / GITHUB_TOKEN) — one App can serve both, but the vars are distinct.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=              # numeric GitHub App id
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=     # App private key (PEM) — server-side secret, never in a sandbox
# GITHUB_APP_SLUG=            # the App's slug; supplies botName (the @mention target) → "<slug>[bot]"
#
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET HMAC-verifies inbound deliveries (X-Hub-Signature-256). REQUIRED:
# if unset, the route REJECTS every delivery (401, fail-closed — this ingress fires
# routines into Slack channels AND wakes coding sessions). The escape hatch for local smoke
# tests of the fan-out without a secret is the `webhooks.allowUnverifiedGithub` SETTING in
# agent/settings/settings.ts (OPE-1153) — no environment variable lowers this wall.
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Slack surface (agent/channels/slack.ts is now active) ---
# The Slack channel binds REAL identity: it reads team_id / channel_id / channel_type
# / is_private / user_id off the inbound event into the session auth context, so
# memory + access + routines key off the real workspace/surface/mode/visibility
# instead of the OE_* stand-ins below. Credentials fall back to these env vars
# (or re-stamp the credential's broker — e.g. `db` for chat-pasted keys — later
# without touching identity):
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=          # xoxb-… bot token (Slack app → OAuth & Permissions)
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=     # Slack app → Basic Information → Signing Secret (verifies webhooks)
# Slack app event subscriptions: `app_mention` (mentions) and, for DMs, `message.im`
# with the `im:history` scope. Webhook route defaults to /eve/v1/slack.

# --- Session lifecycle / rotation — NOT ENV VARS (OPE-411) ---
# eve terminates a durable session at ~40M cumulative INPUT tokens. Three guards keep OE
# comfortably under that, and ALL of them are TYPED SETTINGS in `agent/settings.ts`, not
# environment variables. Change one in a reviewed diff and deploy; see the agent-settings
# table in docs/deploy/configuration-reference.md.
#  1. The always-on channel SUPERVISOR rotates to a fresh generation before it gets close.
#     Since OPE-345 the PRIMARY triggers are about CONTEXT FRESHNESS, not size:
#     `rotation.expiryDays` (7d, `0` disables) and `rotation.quietGapHours` (12h). The two
#     counters behind them are BACKSTOPS: `rotation.tokenBudget` (4,000,000 measured
#     cumulative input tokens per generation) and `rotation.turnBudget` (60 turns). The turn
#     backstop must clear BOTH walls on its own: the newer, lower one is the underlying
#     workflow run's 25,000-event cap (~85 turns at OE's measured ~295 events/turn), and the
#     original is C(budget) < 40M cumulative input (C(60)≈4.9M ✓; the pre-OPE-345 150 cleared
#     40M but sat ABOVE the event wall, and 300 fails both). RE-DERIVE it against a
#     re-measured events-per-turn figure — never nudge it. Both bounds are pinned in
#     packages/core/scripts/rotation-trigger-test.ts.
#  2. `agent.sessionInputTokenBudget` (30,000,000) — eve's own per-session input cap, set
#     BELOW the hosted 40M so a conversation session PARKS on eve's Continue/Stop prompt
#     in-thread (user-visible) instead of dying silently. Keep it under 40M.
#  3. The per-TURN step BOUNDARY: `turn.stepBudget` (120 round-trips — past it, every model
#     call carries a wrap-up instruction; the turn is NOT killed) and
#     `turn.stepBackstopMultiple` (3x — past 360, a text-only stub ends the turn).

# --- Second surface: Linear (agent/channels/linear.ts) ---
# Linear Agent Session webhooks. The channel captures organization/team/issue/actor off
# the event into auth attributes; the binding (bindLinear) maps workspace=org, surface=TEAM
# (the silo grain — many issues in a team share one memory silo; team_id is dug from the raw
# webhook and falls back to the issue when absent), mode=channel. Visibility is enriched from
# the Linear API at dispatch (team.private → public/private); with NO token set it FAILS CLOSED
# to private (surface-tier memory only) — the access token below is what unlocks team-visible
# memory flowing up to the workspace brain.
# Webhook route defaults to /eve/v1/linear. Configure the agent in Linear → Settings →
# API → Agents (OAuth) and set the webhook signing secret here.
# LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Agent access token — used BOTH to post Agent Activities back AND to read team privacy
# (team.private) for the visibility enrichment. Any of these names is accepted (resolved by
# eve's callLinearGraphQL, in this order): LINEAR_AGENT_ACCESS_TOKEN, LINEAR_ACCESS_TOKEN,
# LINEAR_API_KEY, LINEAR_API_TOKEN. Absent → enrichment fails closed (private).
# LINEAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# A SECOND Linear workspace (optional) — a Linear access token is workspace-scoped and an OAuth
# application is private to its own workspace, so each extra workspace gets its own application.
# Register one with four variables; <SLOT> is any label you pick ([A-Z0-9]+, e.g. SOAK):
#   LINEAR_APP_<SLOT>_ORG_ID=          the workspace's Linear organization id
#   LINEAR_APP_<SLOT>_CLIENT_ID=       that application's OAuth client id
#   LINEAR_APP_<SLOT>_CLIENT_SECRET=   that application's OAuth client secret
#   LINEAR_APP_<SLOT>_WEBHOOK_SECRET=  that application's webhook signing secret
# See docs/deploy/surfaces/linear.md Step 2b. Optionally declare the PRIMARY application's own
# organization id — set, an unregistered organization fails closed instead of being served the
# primary credentials.
# LINEAR_ORGANIZATION_ID=

# --- Surface: Microsoft Teams (agent/channels/teams.ts) ---
# Bot Framework Activity protocol. bindTeams maps workspace=tenant, surface=conversation,
# mode (personal→dm / groupChat→mpim / channel→channel); visibility FAILS CLOSED — a team
# channel is only "public" if membershipType=="standard" (and inbound activities usually
# omit it, so channels default private until a Graph-API enrichment lands). Webhook route
# defaults to /eve/v1/teams — point your Azure Bot / Teams app messaging endpoint there.
# Register an Azure AD / Entra app (Bot Channels Registration).
# MICROSOFT_APP_ID=
# MICROSOFT_APP_PASSWORD=
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=        # optional, single-tenant bots
#
# The Teams surfaces you vouch as INTERNAL are declared in agent/identity/surfaces.ts, the same
# config-as-code file every other surface uses — no per-surface Teams vouch is an env var. Declare
# the TENANT ("teams/<tenantId>"); a declaration counts at any rung, so that covers every room under
# it. A member of a cross-surface single window ALSO needs its exact channel key until OPE-1521 makes
# the window floor walk the rungs. See docs/deploy/surfaces/teams.md.

# --- Surface: Discord (agent/channels/discord.ts) ---
# INTERACTION-based: the agent is invoked via a SLASH COMMAND, not an @mention (Eve's
# Discord channel has no message-mention hook). bindDiscord maps workspace=guild (DM→the
# caller's own id, isolated), surface=channel; visibility FAILS CLOSED (guild channels →
# private unless a future @everyone-permission enrichment confirms public). Create a Discord
# application + bot; register the slash command; set the interactions endpoint to /eve/v1/discord.
# The "/oe" command needs no operator step: the deployment reconciles it against Discord at every
# process start, into the guilds agent/commands/discord-commands.ts declares (a guild id is tracked
# config, never a variable). That reconcile needs BOTH names below; either absent and it is skipped.
# DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID=     # which application this deployment speaks as
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=         # verifies signed interaction POSTs

# --- Phase 1: per-channel memory (files-first) ---
# Durable host-side store location. Defaults to ./.open-everywhere-memory (gitignored).
# OE_MEMORY_DIR=/abs/path/to/store
#
# Durable backend (the MemoryProvider seam). NOT REQUIRED (OPE-2230): with DATABASE_URL set the
# backing is "postgres"; with it unset the backing is "git". Set this only to override that:
# "git" (transitional), "sqlite" (real DELETE erasure + at-rest org_id partitioning; Node-built-in,
# no new dependency) or "postgres" (hosted/Neon; register a client at startup).
# OE_MEMORY_PROVIDER=git
#   - sqlite DB file. Backs BOTH memory (when provider=sqlite) AND the operational
#     stores. Defaults to <OE_MEMORY_DIR>/open-everywhere.sqlite3 (gitignored).
# OE_SQLITE_PATH=/abs/path/to/open-everywhere.sqlite3
#
# Operational stores (spend / routines / audit) are DB-backed. NOT REQUIRED (OPE-2230): with
# DATABASE_URL set the store is hosted "postgres"; with it unset the store is the embedded
# Postgres (PGlite) floor. Set this only to override that. "sqlite" is DEPRECATED and is now
# reachable ONLY by naming it here; boot nags when it resolves.
# OE_OPERATIONAL_PROVIDER=sqlite
#
# 🔴 THE DEPLOYMENT'S OWN IDENTITY IS NOT AN ENV VAR (OPE-1545). The tenant org, the ambient
# workspace and the ambient surface are TRACKED CONFIG: declare them in `agent/identity/surfaces.ts`
# (the `deploymentIdentity` object) and change them by PR. A tenant id and a room key are literals a
# reviewer must be able to read in Git, so `OE_ORG`, `OE_WORKSPACE`, `OE_SURFACE` and the older
# `OE_CHANNEL` spelling are gone — nothing reads them, and there is no fallback. Setting one now
# does nothing at all.
#
# The ambient workspace/surface pair is the `eve dev` (HTTP, no surface) gap-filler: it keeps the
# partition key STABLE across local runs so memory persists. Every wired surface derives the real
# (platform, workspace, surface) from the inbound event and ignores the pair.
# (OE_PLATFORM is deleted — OPE-2411. The host is Vercel's own `VERCEL`; the sandbox choice is the
# `sandbox.selfHostBackend` / `sandbox.useVercelSandbox` settings.)
# Surface visibility. A private surface READS shared workspace/domain memory but
# WRITES only to its own surface tier (its learnings never reach the workspace).
# Set by the Slack adapter from the channel type; locally:
# OE_VISIBILITY=public   # or "private"

# --- Phase 2: identity & access (config-as-code in agent/access/bundles.ts) ---
# Session mode for local testing — the Slack adapter sets this later.
#   channel = service identity | dm = caller's personal universe | mpim = execution-only (no learning)
# OE_MODE=channel
# Calling principal, for DM-mode testing (DM resolves against dm:<principal> bundles only).
# OE_PRINCIPAL=U_you
# Egress firewall posture — TYPED SETTINGS, NOT ENV VARS (OPE-411). `egress.enforce` (true)
# applies the compiled default-deny policy with credential injection; set it false in
# `agent/settings.ts` to opt out to dry-run (compiles + logs the policy, doesn't apply it)
# while bundles don't yet cover every host the agent needs. Fine-grained policy only applies
# on the Vercel sandbox backend (Docker = coarse; just-bash rejects it) — on a backend that
# can't apply it the session ABORTS (fail-closed; eve's default is allow-all).
# OFF VERCEL, choosing an egress posture is a CONSCIOUS decision: if you wire NO proxy
# (OE_EGRESS_PROXY_URL below) and leave `egress.allowUnenforced` at its shipped false, the app
# boots but emits a LOUD boot warning and then aborts EVERY session at session.started
# (fail-closed). Declare `egress: { allowUnenforced: true }` only to run knowingly WITHOUT a
# firewall in dev — you'll get a persistent "UNENFORCED egress" boot warning. The enforced path
# is to wire the proxy sidecar instead (see just below).
#
# Self-host (Vercel-less) egress enforcement — the forced local proxy (deploy/egress-proxy,
# docker-compose.yml). OFF Vercel, instead of Vercel's server-side firewall, the compiled
# policy is PUSHED to the proxy control service and enforced there (Tier A host allow/deny;
# Tier B MITM adds method/path narrowing + credential injection). With this wired,
# `egress.allowUnenforced` is NOT needed — the proxy applies the policy natively.
# See docs/deploy/platforms/self-host-egress.md.
#   OE_EGRESS_PROXY_URL   — control endpoint the app pushes policy to (compose sets it)
# OE_EGRESS_PROXY_URL=http://oe-egress-proxy:8080
#   OE_EGRESS_PROXY_TOKEN — REQUIRED shared secret gating POST/DELETE /policy; the proxy
#                           control service refuses to boot without it (generate one:
#                           openssl rand -hex 32) and compose fails fast if it is unset
OE_EGRESS_PROXY_TOKEN=
#   OE_EGRESS_MITM        — 1 = Tier B (local-CA MITM); default 0 = Tier A (no decryption)
# OE_EGRESS_MITM=0
#   The off-Vercel execution backend is the `sandbox.selfHostBackend` SETTING (OPE-2411 deleted
#     OE_SANDBOX_BACKEND). Shipped `docker` contains the agent in a per-session container;
#     `justbash` runs in-process and SHARES THIS APP PROCESS'S FILESYSTEM, for dev and testing only.
#     This repository's own app declares `justbash` in agent/settings/settings.ts, which is what
#     local dev, the Docker image and the integration tier all run.
#   EVE_DOCKER_PATH       — eve's own variable, read here too. Path to a Docker-compatible CLI
#     (e.g. podman) when it is not on PATH as `docker`. eve uses it to run the sandbox container;
#     the turn-end stop hook uses the SAME binary to stop it. Unset = plain `docker`.
# EVE_DOCKER_PATH=
#
# BASH HANG GUARD (agent/stall/bash-hang-guard.ts) — both layers armed by default, any backend.
# `bash` resolves when the command's OUTPUT STREAM closes, not when its process exits, so a
# backgrounded job still holding stdout/stderr keeps the call open indefinitely (measured: 753s,
# zero bytes, still open when the probe gave up).
# Both are TYPED SETTINGS in `agent/settings.ts`, not env vars (OPE-411).
#   `bash.backgroundGuard` (true) — layer 1: refuse a bare `… &` with neither stream redirected
#     BEFORE it runs, handing back the corrected form (`… > /tmp/out.log 2>&1 &`). Nothing is
#     rewritten silently. Ambiguity (heredocs, `wait`) fails OPEN.
#   `bash.timeoutMs` (600000) — layer 2: wall-clock cap on ONE bash call; on a trip the model gets
#     a diagnostic naming the likely cause instead of an opaque `terminated`. `0` disables it.
#     The 600000 default is a BACKSTOP for hang shapes layer 1 can't parse, not a budget for long
#     work — background that to a log and poll it. It was 240000, picked against a believed ~341s
#     platform ceiling that measurement could not reproduce (laptop, then production), so it now
#     clears the longest foreground command proven to complete (~400s) and stays well under the
#     sandbox session window. See the DEFAULT_BASH_TIMEOUT_MS docblock for the evidence.
#
# Each connection in bundles.ts names a `secretRef` — an env var holding its secret,
# e.g. a Linear bearer key. Never inline secrets in bundles.ts.
# LINEAR_API_KEY=

# --- Connector plane: host-side Eve connections (agent/connections/*.ts) ---
# A connection's tools are called HOST-SIDE (Eve attaches the token before the
# request leaves the host); these calls bypass the sandbox egress firewall. A bundle
# grants a connection by NAME (`connectors: ["notion"]` in bundles.ts); the resolver
# scopes + audits the grant. The shipped agent/connections/notion.ts reads this token
# (a Notion internal-integration token) via its app-principal getToken. For OAuth instead
# of a static key, the built-in own-rolled OAuth flow (oe-oauth) mints/refreshes host-side.
# NOT an env var (OPE-408 step 2) — the shipped `team-key` credential is stamped
# `broker: "db"`. Load it with: oe secret set db:notion:team-key
#   Shared user-OAuth vault (C.3, review/design/c3-oauth-vault-threat-model.md) — the
#   `connectors.linkSetVault` TYPED SETTING, not an env var (OPE-411); default ON (OPE-2414).
#   The CREDENTIAL half requires the per-user credential path (`connectors.userPrincipal`) to
#   be armed too (it ships armed); on its own it mints no shared token. It is NOT inert on its own, though —
#   arming it alone still suppresses directory auto-confirm for EVERY link-set (see
#   `directory.autoConfirm` below), so the guard cannot fail open on a flag pairing a future
#   transport might not honour. Armed, a caller who is a confirmed member of an active link-set keys
#   the own-rolled OAuth vault by their org-scoped LINK-SET subject instead of their raw platform
#   principal id — so the same user-OAuth token is reused across all of that person's confirmed
#   linked DMs (one consent, not one per account). Unlinked/unconfirmed callers are byte-identical
#   to today.

# --- Surface: Notion comments (agent/channels/notion.ts) — INTERIM comment-bot ---
# Lets the agent be @mentioned in a Notion page/discussion comment and reply into the same
# thread. Custom defineChannel over Notion's REST API + a SEPARATE channel-plumbing integration
# credential, db:notion:channel-key (the comment retrieve/create + users/me + channel page/comment
# reads). This is DISTINCT from db:notion:team-key above (the work-surface egress key): they are
# separate credentials even if you store the same integration token under both — load BOTH to run
# the comment surface AND the egress connection. No fallback: if db:notion:channel-key has no
# stored value the comment surface fails closed. Point a Notion webhook subscription at
# POST /eve/v1/notion. NOT an env var (OPE-408 step 2) — load it with:
# oe secret set db:notion:channel-key
#
#   Loop: comment.created webhook (a SIGNAL — no text) -> GET /v1/comments/{id} -> parse
#   rich-text mentions -> dispatch ONLY when the bot is @mentioned -> POST /v1/comments back
#   into the discussion. bindNotion maps workspace=workspace_id, surface=page_id (the silo),
#   discussion=reply anchor; visibility FAILS CLOSED (private) and trust FAILS CLOSED
#   (external) — the interim "paired knowledge surface" tier, OE-audited-only (Notion has no
#   integration-reachable audit). Superseded when the External Agents API (waitlist) lands.
#
# NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET is the one-time `verification_token` Notion sends to the endpoint when
# you create the subscription. It is the PERMANENT HMAC key for every delivery
# (X-Notion-Signature = sha256 of the raw body), not a bootstrap nonce. REQUIRED: unset ->
# every delivery is rejected 401 (fail-closed). The escape hatch for local smoke tests is the
# `webhooks.allowUnverifiedNotion` SETTING in agent/settings/settings.ts (OPE-1153) — no
# environment variable lowers this wall.
#
#   HOW YOU GET IT — the token is NEVER printed to the logs (it's a signing key; a logged one is
#   forgeable by anyone with log access for as long as logs are retained). On the handshake POST
#   the channel stores it encrypted and logs a redacted line carrying a sha256 fingerprint and a
#   ONE-SHOT, 15-minute reveal link:
#
#     [open-everywhere:notion] handshake secret for Notion webhook (verification_token) received
#     and STORED — NOT logged (sha256 fingerprint 1a2b3c4d; …). Read it ONCE here, within 15
#     minutes …: https://<your-deploy-url>/connect/v1/reveal?t=…
#
#   Open that link, copy the value, then do BOTH: paste it into Notion's verify form and load it
#   with `oe secret set NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET` (the credential store is its home; this env var is
#   the migration on-ramp and the stored value wins). THE LINK IS THE SECRET while it lives — anyone who opens it first gets the value, so
#   treat the log line as sensitive until you've redeemed it. Missed the window, or lost the
#   value? Delete and recreate the Notion subscription; there is no re-reveal by design.
#   Confirm what you pasted matches without exposing it:
#     printf %s "$NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET" | sha256sum | cut -c1-8   # == the logged fingerprint
#   The reveal page needs OE_INTAKE_PAGE_SIGNING_KEY + OE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL set (both in
#   docs/deploy/configuration-reference.md; the signing key arms BOTH page routes). Without
#   them the token is still captured, still not logged, and the log tells you to configure them
#   and re-run the handshake.
#   RUNNING SEVERAL NOTION INTEGRATIONS? A domain can bind its own (memory domain `credentials`,
#   docs/deploy/surfaces/notion.md Step 4). Each integration issues its OWN verification_token, so
#   each gets its own operator secret and the domain names it with `inboundSecret`. OE tries every
#   configured integration's secret and the one that verifies identifies the sender.
# NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# NOTION_BOT_USER_ID=   # optional: the integration's own user id (else resolved via /v1/users/me
#                       # and cached). It's the id a bot @mention carries + the self-reply loop guard.

# --- Phase 3: spend caps (config-as-code in agent/spend/caps.ts) ---
# The durable token ledger is DB-backed (see OE_OPERATIONAL_PROVIDER above).
# Per-surface / per-workspace monthly caps are set in caps.ts (uncapped by default).
# When a scope is over budget Open Everywhere DECLINES new work — DMs included. View: npm run spend.

# --- Per-session cost + usage analytics (agent/spend/usage-events.ts, pricing.ts) ---
# Row-level sibling of the ledger above: powers session_cost / spend_report / usage_report
# (in-conversation agent tools) and the control plane's usage_summary. The retention window is
# the `spend.usageEventsTtlDays` TYPED SETTING (90 days; opportunistic prune on write), not an env
# var (OPE-411). Only the deprecated SQLite floor prunes. On Postgres, PGlite included, the prune is a
# documented no-op and the rows stay until your own retention on the database removes them (see
# docs/security/spend-and-cost.md).
#
# Model pricing is NOT an env var (OPE-1148). Rates are config-as-code in
# `agent/spend/model-pricing.ts`, and `npm run lint:model-ids` fails CI when a model this config
# can select has no committed rate — see docs/security/spend-and-cost.md.
#
# Sandbox $ estimate assumptions live in `agent/settings.ts` as `spend.sandboxMemoryGiB` (4) and
# `spend.sandboxGiBHourUsd` (0.0212), verified against Vercel's published rate + a real bill — only
# change them if your deploy pins a non-default sandbox memory size or the rate moves. They are
# typed settings rather than env vars precisely BECAUSE they are evidence: a reviewer should see the
# number move in the same diff as the evidence for it (OPE-411).

# --- Runtime domain skills (agent/skills/domain-skills.ts) ---
# Any member of a memory domain can author/update/delete a skill in-thread (create_skill /
# list_skills / delete_skill) — no code, no deploy, audited, armed from the next turn.
# The per-domain count cap is the `skills.domainSkillCap` typed setting (50); each skill is
# separately capped at 32KB, which is not configurable.

# --- Append-only audit log (agent/audit/) ---
# Records who/what/where/when for memory writes, egress + connector grants, and
# consequential tool actions (pr.open, routine.create/cancel). DB-backed; append-only
# by design (no delete — outside the memory-erasure path; Postgres enforces it at the
# DB layer via SELECT/INSERT-only RLS). View: npm run audit [--org X --action Y --since ISO].

# --- Identity directory sync: pre-populate identity links from an IdP (Okta) ---
# OFF by default and OFF unless armed here. The IdP is a SYNC SOURCE, never a live
# dependency and never a merge authority: nothing on a request path calls it, and
# everything it proposes lands as a `suggested` link — invisible to attribution until
# the person taps confirm in their own DM (`confirm_link`). A person the directory
# reports as DEPROVISIONED has their link-set SUSPENDED eagerly (narrowing is safe to
# automate); nothing is ever unlinked or revoked by a sync. Runs daily on a schedule
# (agent/schedules/directory-sync.ts) plus the `directory_sync_now` control-plane verb.
# OE_DIRECTORY_PROVIDER=okta          # unset = off; "okta" arms the adapter
# OE_DIRECTORY_OKTA_ORG_URL=https://acme.okta.com
#   The API token is a credential REF resolved through a BROKER — never a raw value
#   here. Default broker "db" (OPE-408 step 2): name any ref you like and load its value
#   with `oe secret set <ref>` — no env var needed at all. Point the broker at "env"
#   instead to keep the token in a plain env var (the ref then names that var's name).
# OE_DIRECTORY_OKTA_TOKEN_REF=db:okta:token
# OE_DIRECTORY_OKTA_TOKEN_BROKER=db
#   The sync's three POLICY choices are settings in agent/settings/settings.ts, not env
#   (OPE-2411 deleted OE_DIRECTORY_OKTA_APPS, OE_DIRECTORY_AUTOCONFIRM and
#   OE_DIRECTORY_GROUP_ALLOWLIST): `directory.oktaApps` (which Okta app maps to which of our
#   platforms), `directory.autoConfirm` (the single escape from "a human confirms every link")
#   and `directory.groupAllowlist` (which IdP groups sync). Which orgs a sweep covers is
#   `schedules.sweepOrgs` (was OE_TIDY_ORGS). docs/deploy/configuration-reference.md has each row.

# --- Control plane: remote admin auth (agent/control/auth.ts) ---
# The remote MCP control surface is a RESOURCE server — it validates a bearer token,
# never issues one. Auth is federated + pluggable by ISSUER (one generic OIDC verifier
# covers Google/Auth0/Okta/Entra/WorkOS). FAIL-CLOSED: with none of these set, the
# remote handler refuses every request (401). The local stdio entry needs no auth.
#
# Generic OIDC — point at ANY OIDC issuer; "different users use what they want" is just
# this URL. (GitHub is OAuth2, not OIDC, for user login — declare `control.githubAdmin: true`.)
# OE_ADMIN_OIDC_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# OE_ADMIN_OIDC_AUDIENCE=<your-oauth-client-id>
# OE_ADMIN_STATIC_TOKEN=<secret>    # single-operator self-host (token == admin)
#
# Role rules — who gets which roles — are the `control.adminRoleRules` setting in
# agent/settings/settings.ts (OPE-2411 deleted the OE_ADMIN_ROLE_RULES JSON). Who may administer the
# deployment is policy, so it changes by a reviewed commit.
